No -Wl,--no-undefined / -Wl,-undefined,error on OpenBSD.

OpenBSD's clang links libc into executables, not shared libraries, so every libc
symbol is undefined in a shared library: libQt6Core.so failed to link with
"ld: error: undefined symbol: strlen" (openbsd populate 2026-10-24).  Presetting
the probes off only trades that for Qt's FATAL_ERROR ("Platform linker doesn't
support erroring upon encountering undefined symbols"), so skip the helper there,
as OpenBSD's own Qt port does.  The helper is installed with Qt, so the add-on
modules (qtmultimedia, qtshadertools) get the same behaviour.

--- a/cmake/QtFlagHandlingHelpers.cmake
+++ b/cmake/QtFlagHandlingHelpers.cmake
@@ -159,6 +159,11 @@
         # passing this option is deprecated, causing a warning.
         return()
     endif()
+    if(CMAKE_SYSTEM_NAME STREQUAL "OpenBSD")
+        # OpenBSD links libc into executables, not shared libraries, so every
+        # libc symbol is undefined in a shared library and --no-undefined fails.
+        return()
+    endif()
     if ((GCC OR CLANG) AND NOT MSVC)
         if(CLANG AND QT_FEATURE_sanitizer)
             return()
